 ## Background
-The optimization of the 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterization pipeline emerged alongside the initial publication of 3DGS [Parent](2812-3d-gaussian-splatting.md). As 3DGS gained popularity for novel view synthesis and SLAM, the computational cost of rendering millions of Gaussians became a primary bottleneck. Early implementations used coarse bounding boxes for culling, leading to significant overdraw. Subsequent works like Speedy-Splat [S1, unofficial] and AdR-Gaussian [S1, unofficial] introduced precise geometric culling and load balancing. Concurrently, hardware-oriented approaches [S2] explored FPGA and ASIC implementations to accelerate the specific mathematical operations of Gaussian projection and sorting. The technique is widely used in real-time rendering applications, SLAM systems (e.g., SplaTAM variants), and neural rendering pipelines where frame rate is critical.
